# Runtime output, never repo content. These tools write logs beside the checkout
# they run in, so any branch worked on in a live checkout accumulates them: a
# feature branch here once carried 44 stray log files and a reviewer had to
# point it out. The `latest` symlinks move on every invocation, which also makes
# them a standing conflict between concurrent branches.
tending/

# The smoke job builds the binary into the workspace root, so anyone
# reproducing those steps locally leaves an untracked executable behind — and
# it would become a nocode finding the day this repo gates itself.
/nova-check
/nova-check.exe

# A repo-root bin/ is where a local `go build -o bin/<tool> ./cmd/<tool>` lands,
# and one of those executables was tracked for a while: a 4.9 MB Mach-O arm64
# `bin/nova-bus`, swept in by an unrelated commit, referenced by nothing, one
# platform only, and carrying no version stamp anything here could identify. A
# source repository ships no built executables — a reader cannot tell what is
# inside one, and a release tarball that carries one is a supply-chain surface
# with no provenance. Build them; do not commit them.
/bin/
